---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

export const prerender = true;
const UPDATED = '2026-11-07';
const repo = 'https://github.com/MgnCoding2020/burn-and-churn-website';
---

<Base
  title="Privacy"
  description="What data the Burn and Churn Coffee portfolio site handles, and what it doesn't."
>
  <article class="container prose">
    <span class="eyebrow">Privacy</span>
    <h1>What this site knows about you</h1>
    <p class="updated">Last updated {UPDATED}</p>
    <p class="lede">
      Very little. Burn and Churn Coffee is a fictional business and this site is a portfolio
      project. The public pages have no accounts, no forms, no cookies, no analytics and no
      trackers. Only the staff portal, used by the café's fictional test employees, has sign-in and
      cookies.
    </p>

    <h2>What is collected today</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">What</th>
          <th scope="col">Details</th>
          <th scope="col">Kept for</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Request logs (our code)</td>
          <td>
            Method, path, response status, timing and a random request ID. No query strings, cookies
            or request bodies.
          </td>
          <td>3 days</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Request logs (Cloudflare, our host)</td>
          <td>
            Cloudflare's own record of each request and response, which can include connection
            details such as your IP address and approximate location. Query strings are removed from
            these logs.
          </td>
          <td>3 days</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Rate-limit counts (Cloudflare)</td>
          <td>
            For requests to the site's API and sign-in only: a count per IP address, so one visitor
            can't flood the site. Counts cover one-minute windows. Cloudflare keeps them; this site
            doesn't store them.
          </td>
          <td>1 minute</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Staff accounts (staff portal only)</td>
          <td>
            For test employee accounts only: the name and role from Microsoft Entra ID, the
            account's directory ID, and when it first and last signed in.
          </td>
          <td>Project life</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>
            Staff session cookie <code>__Host-bc_session</code>
          </td>
          <td>
            A random value that keeps a staff member signed in. The server stores only a hash of it,
            with its expiry time. Strictly necessary; set only after staff sign-in. It ends at
            sign-out, after 1 hour without use, or after 8 hours, and is deleted from the server
            within the hour after.
          </td>
          <td>8 hours</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>
            Sign-in cookie <code>__Host-bc_login</code>
          </td>
          <td>
            The one-time values of a staff sign-in in progress, encrypted so the browser can't read
            or change them. It ties the sign-in to the browser that started it, so a sign-in can't
            be forced on someone else. Strictly necessary; deleted when the sign-in finishes.
          </td>
          <td>10 minutes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Counter orders (staff portal)</td>
          <td>
            For the fictional café's test sales: the drinks, prices, cash handed over and change,
            and which staff member took the payment. No card details, ever.
          </td>
          <td>90 days</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Staff audit log</td>
          <td>
            Each staff sign-in and sign-out, each refused sign-in of a real work account (with the
            reason), each sale, and each sold-out change or restock request, with who did it and the
            request ID. Staff see their own entries and those of roles below theirs. It can't be
            edited or deleted, by design. Other failed sign-ins appear only in the request logs.
          </td>
          <td>Project life</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Logs are used only to keep the site working and secure. They're stored in Cloudflare Workers
      Logs, which keeps them for 3 days on the free plan this site uses (checked against
      Cloudflare's documentation on {UPDATED}).
    </p>

    <h2>What this site does not do</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>No cookies on the public pages, and nothing stored in your browser.</li>
      <li>No analytics, advertising or tracking scripts.</li>
      <li>
        No third-party requests: fonts, images and video are served from this site, not from Google
        Fonts or another service.
      </li>
      <li>No real orders or payments, and no card numbers, ever.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What's planned</h2>
    <p>
      Later phases add features that will handle more data. This page will be updated{' '}
      <strong>before</strong> each one goes live, describing exactly what it stores and for how
      long:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Customer sign-in</strong> (Microsoft Entra External ID): one strictly necessary
        session cookie; test accounts only.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Online ordering</strong> (Stripe Checkout in test mode): order details for a
        fictional café; card details stay on Stripe's pages and never reach this site.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>An AI menu helper</strong>: message counts only; conversations are not stored.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Questions</h2>
    <p>
      Customers have no accounts yet, so there's no personal data to request or delete today; staff
      accounts are fictional test accounts. If you have a question or spot something wrong, open an
      issue on <a href={`${repo}/issues`}>GitHub</a>, or report a security concern through{' '}
      <a href={`${repo}/security/advisories/new`}>private vulnerability reporting</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
